ph lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm m=
n mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">OAuth is not authentication. It=
=E2=80=99s an authorization protocol, or, better yet, a delegation protocol=
. It=E2=80=99s for this reason that authentication protocols such as OpenID=
 Connect exist and legacy protocols such as SAML use extension grants to li=
nk authentication and delegation.</p><p id=3D"2b72" class=3D"pw-post-body-p=
aragraph lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk m=
l mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">There are articles on this=
 subject already, but I still regularly see some confusion as to the reason=
s why on the internet and with new clients. So in this article, I=E2=80=99m=
 going to discuss the key reasons why OAuth on its own does not provide cli=
ent applications (relying parties) with user authentication.</p><h1 id=3D"1=
f2a" class=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng n=
h ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Authentication vs. Auth=
orization</h1><p id=3D"dcda" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b =
lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-=
selectable-paragraph=3D"">Before we start, let=E2=80=99s clarify the differ=
ence between authentication and authorization:</p><ul class=3D""><li id=3D"=
7fe9" class=3D"lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb ns md me mf nt mh mi m=
j nu ml mm mn mo nv nw nx bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><strong class=
=3D"lt ew">Authentication</strong> is the process of verifying an identity =
(who they say they are)</li><li id=3D"3630" class=3D"lr ls ev lt b lu ny lw=
 lx ly nz ma mb ns oa me mf nt ob mi mj nu oc mm mn mo nv nw nx bj" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D""><strong class=3D"lt ew">Authorization</strong> is t=
he process of verifying what someone is allowed to do (permissions)</li></u=
l><p id=3D"7d35" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx =
ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-p=
aragraph=3D"">This is how I differentiate these terms in my head. Your mile=
age may vary.</p><h1 id=3D"c882" class=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx =
my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D"">Access Tokens are Not Intended for the Client</h1><p id=3D"e3ab" clas=
s=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me =
mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Let=E2=
=80=99s start with the biggest reason why OAuth isn=E2=80=99t authenticatio=
n: access tokens are not intended for the client application.</p><p id=3D"e=
da2" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb =
mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"=
">When an authorization server issues an access token, <strong class=3D"lt =
ew">the intended audience is the protected resource</strong>. After all, th=
is is what the token is providing access to. It=E2=80=99s down to the prote=
cted resource to understand and validate the token. One of the benefits of =
OAuth is that the protected resource doesn=E2=80=99t even need to know who =
the requesting client application is, just what permissions it has been del=
egated.</p><p id=3D"3c87" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu =
lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo eo bj" data-sel=
ectable-paragraph=3D"">In OAuth, all the client application needs to do is =
to request the token, use it to access the resource, and then handle the re=
sponse (both success and failure).</p><h1 id=3D"20b9" class=3D"mp mq ev be =
mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-=
selectable-paragraph=3D"">Access Tokens do Not Represent a User</h1><p id=
=3D"a717" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no m=
a mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D"">Instead, <strong class=3D"lt ew">an access token represents the dele=
gated permissions</strong> that the resource owner (the user) has given the=
 client application. An access token could contain information about the us=
er who delegated permission, but it does not actually represent the user.</=
p><h1 id=3D"2c09" class=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc =
nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Access Tok=
ens do not Represent Authentication</h1><p id=3D"8b22" class=3D"pw-post-bod=
y-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj m=
k nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">An access token also do=
es not represent or give any indication of a user having authenticated. <st=
rong class=3D"lt ew">There may not even be a user present!</strong> It coul=
d have been a machine using client credentials, or even impersonation using=
 ROPC.</p><h1 id=3D"9233" class=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz n=
a nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Ac=
cess Tokens can be Anything and Contain Anything</h1><p id=3D"931b" class=
=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me m=
f mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">There is=
 no standard structure for an access token. So even though the past two poi=
nts sound like they could be worked around, <strong class=3D"lt ew">there=
=E2=80=99s no guarantee that your client application would be able to under=
stand it</strong>.</p><p id=3D"cd4e" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls =
ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo eo b=
j" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">If you are relying on an access token bei=
ng a structured piece of data, there=E2=80=99s also no reason it can=E2=80=
=99t change its structure at any time. Only the authorization server and re=
lying parties get a say in the access token structure; client applications =
don=E2=80=99t get a say.</p><p id=3D"145d" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph =
lr ls ev lt b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn m=
o eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">As I was writing this, Vittorio Ber=
tocci <a class=3D"af lq" href=3D"http://www.cloudidentity.com/blog/2018/04/=
20/clients-shouldnt-peek-inside-access-tokens/" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollo=
w" target=3D"_blank">wrote a similar article</a> after finding some Microso=
ft Graph API users using Azure access tokens for user authentication, again=
 dispelling some myths around their usage.</p><h1 id=3D"b51b" class=3D"mp m=
q ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm b=
j" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Access Tokens cannot be Safely Verified b=
y the Client Application</h1><p id=3D"5ab6" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph=
 lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn =
mo eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Yes, even if you are using a JWT, =
there is not much hope for a client application to receive access tokens an=
d be sure that no one cut and paste that token from a different request. Th=
is is because you are not the intended audience of the token, and also have=
 <strong class=3D"lt ew">no way of matching the received token to your requ=
est</strong>.</p><p id=3D"f281" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt=
 b lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo eo bj" da=
ta-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This issue is covered in detail by John Bradle=
y in his article <a class=3D"af lq" href=3D"http://www.thread-safe.com/2012=
/01/problem-with-oauth-for-authentication.html" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollo=
w" target=3D"_blank">=E2=80=9CThe problem with OAuth for Authentication=E2=
=80=9D</a>, this time focussing on Facebook Connect.</p><h1 id=3D"9ba9" cla=
ss=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj =
nk nl nm bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">What about Resource Owner Passw=
ord Credentials (ROPC)?</h1><p id=3D"dccc" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph =
lr ls ev lt b lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn m=
o eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><a class=3D"af lq" href=3D"https://=
www.scottbrady91.com/OAuth/Why-the-Resource-Owner-Password-Credentials-Gran=
t-Type-is-not-Authentication-nor-Suitable-for-Modern-Applications" rel=3D"n=
oopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">Don=E2=80=99t get me started on ROP=
C</a>. Just because this grant type contains user credentials, doesn=E2=80=
=99t mean the that the resulting token represents a user, or the act of a u=
ser authenticating. Instead, it is the <strong class=3D"lt ew">impersonatio=
n of the user</strong> by the client application. Take a look at my <a clas=
s=3D"af lq" href=3D"https://www.scottbrady91.com/OAuth/Why-the-Resource-Own=
er-Password-Credentials-Grant-Type-is-not-Authentication-nor-Suitable-for-M=
odern-Applications" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">rant on=
 ROPC</a> for more details.</p><h1 id=3D"140f" class=3D"mp mq ev be mr ms m=
t mu mv mw mx my mz na nb nc nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm bj" data-selecta=
ble-paragraph=3D"">=E2=80=9CAh, but that could be worked around by=E2=80=A6=
=E2=80=9D</h1><p id=3D"3d28" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lr ls ev lt b =
lu nn lw lx ly no ma mb mc np me mf mg nq mi mj mk nr mm mn mo eo bj" data-=
selectable-paragraph=3D"">If your answer to any of the above points starts =
with the above statement: Stop and take a look into OpenID Connect. OpenID =
Connect is an authentication protocol built on top of OAuth, is already wid=
ely adopted, and really does offer solutions to each of the points above. P=
lease don=E2=80=99t make another <a class=3D"af lq" href=3D"https://www.sco=
ttbrady91.com/Blockchain-Identity/Technical-Review-of-Civics-Secure-Identit=
y-Platform" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">MyOwnOAuth=E2=
=84=A2</a>.</p></div></div></div><div class=3D"ab ca od oe of og" role=3D"s=
